---
/**
 * ============================================================================
 * PROJECTS SECTION (Projects.astro)
 * ============================================================================
 * What this component does:
 * - Highlights your key software projects in a clean two-column responsive grid.
 *
 * HOW TO MAINTAIN:
 * - To edit an existing project: update the name, link, image, or bullet points
 *   inside any <article class="project-card">.
 * - To add a new project: copy an entire <article class="project-card"> block
 *   and paste it inside <div class="projects-grid">.
 * - Images can be placed in `public/` and linked via `<img src="/your-image.png" />`.
 * ============================================================================
 */
---

<section id="resume-projects" class="story-section container">
  <h2>Projects</h2>
  <p>
    This section highlights projects that reflect how I think, build, and
    deliver practical solutions.
  </p>
  <div class="projects-grid">
    <!-- PROJECT CARD 1 -->
    <article class="project-card">
      <div class="project-media project-media-brand" role="img" aria-label="TaskLocal Trust and Safety">
        <div class="tasklocal-lockup">
          <p class="tasklocal-mark">TaskLocal</p>
          <p class="tasklocal-name">Trust &amp; Safety</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="project-content">
        <h3>TaskLocal Trust &amp; Safety</h3>
        <p class="project-links">
          <a
            href="https://task-local-trust-and-safety.vercel.app"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >Live demo</a
          >
          <a
            href="https://github.com/justin-day-pursuit/TaskLocal-TrustAndSafety"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >GitHub</a
          >
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            TaskLocal is a local-services marketplace. When a customer or
            provider reports a review, a reviewer has to decide what needs
            attention today, and reading every comment by hand does not scale.
          </li>
          <li>
            The Trust &amp; Safety desk is that morning work surface. Three
            cards — new reports today, unhandled reports, and the high-risk
            case — open one shared list, and a briefing covers what changed
            since the last report. The day resets at a 9:00 AM cutoff.
          </li>
          <li>
            Emails, phone numbers, URLs, handles, and long digit strings are
            removed before any review text reaches the model. Chart values are
            counted in the app. Database calls stop after 10 seconds, and a
            full analysis stops after 90 seconds. A failed run leaves the last
            good report on screen.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- PROJECT CARD 2 -->
    <article class="project-card">
      <div class="project-media">
        <img
          src="/professor-fritts.png"
          alt="Professor Fritts poster: a stylized portrait beside a glass building, with the words Renewable Energy Planning"
        />
      </div>
      <div class="project-content">
        <h3>Professor Fritts</h3>
        <p class="project-links">
          <a
            href="https://professor-fritts-clean-energy.vercel.app"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >Live demo</a
          >
          <a
            href="https://github.com/justin-day-pursuit/professor-fritts-clean-energy"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >GitHub</a
          >
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Facility directors at New York City schools, libraries, and other
            municipal buildings face Local Law 97 carbon limits. A first-pass
            feasibility study often takes 3 to 6 months and tens of thousands
            of dollars before anyone knows whether solar, storage, or
            efficiency work is viable.
          </li>
          <li>
            Professor Fritts is a pre-engineering advisor. From a building
            address, a capital budget, and an energy goal, it looks up the tax
            lot and rooftop solar potential and returns three scenarios side by
            side: solar only, solar plus battery backup, and solar plus storage
            plus efficiency upgrades. A full plan takes about 2 minutes.
          </li>
          <li>
            The plan runs 13 sections, including cost, carbon, payback, and the
            licensed reviews required before public money is spent. Every
            figure is tagged as a fact, an estimate, an assumption, or a
            recommendation. Requests outside the five boroughs, or any wording
            that promises guaranteed savings, are turned away before a plan is
            shown.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>
